{0 Getting started}

This page shows you how to get a small GTK 4 sample application running from scratch.

{1 Requirements}

- An opam switch with OCaml 4.14.2 or later installed
- {b Operating System}: Linux or macOS (Windows is not supported at this time; ocgtk remains untested on the BSDs)

{1 Install}

{2 System dependencies}

opam can install system dependencies itself on some platforms, but it's usually a good idea to do this yourself. On most platforms, this is the GTK 4 development package.

For example, on Debian/Ubuntu, Fedora and macOS systems:

{@sh[
sudo apt-get install libgtk-4-dev     # Debian, Ubuntu
sudo dnf install gtk4-devel           # Fedora
brew install gtk4                     # macOS (Homebrew)
]}

{2 Installing ocgtk}

[ocgtk] is one of a group of related packages, one per library, e.g. [ocgtk] for GTK 4, [ocgdk] for GDK and [ocpango] for Pango. There are also corresponding [conf-*] packages, which ensure that the C dependencies (from the previous step) are installed (see {{!page-conventions.section-libraries}Libraries and namespaces}).

{3 From opam}

{@sh[
opam install ocgtk
]}

{3 From the ocgtk repository}

You will need to pin the repository's packages first, then install [ocgtk]:

{@sh[
git clone https://github.com/chris-armstrong/ocgtk.git
cd ocgtk
for p in conf-*/; do opam pin add -n -y "${p%/}" "./$p"; done
opam pin add -n -y .
opam install ocgtk
]}

{1 Create a project}

Create a directory with three files:

{@text[
counter/
  dune-project
  dune
  counter.ml
]}

[dune-project]:

{@dune[
(lang dune 3.0)
]}

[dune]:

{@dune[
(executable
 (name counter)
 (libraries ocgtk))
]}

[counter.ml]:

{@ocaml[
open Ocgtk

let activate app =
  (* Create an ApplicationWindow and set its title and size *)
  let window = Application_window.new_ app in
  window#set_title (Some "Counter");
  window#set_default_size 300 200;

  (* Set up a counter, a label and a button *)
  let count = ref 0 in
  let label = Label.new_ (Some "Clicked 0 times") in
  let button = Button.new_with_label "Click me" in

  (* `ignore` just ignores the result of button#on_clicked -
     you can also do `let _ = button#on_clicked ... in ...` *)
  ignore
    (button#on_clicked
       ~callback:(fun () ->
         incr count;
         label#set_label (Printf.sprintf "Clicked %d times" !count))
       ());

  let box = Box.new_ `VERTICAL 12 in
  box#set_margin_top 12;
  box#set_margin_bottom 12;
  box#set_margin_start 12;
  box#set_margin_end 12;
  (* When appending the label and button, coerce them to the
     Widget.widget_t type *)
  box#append (label :> Widget.widget_t);
  box#append (button :> Widget.widget_t);

  window#set_child (Some (box :> Widget.widget_t));
  window#present ()

let () =
  let app = Application.new_ (Some "org.example.Counter") [ `DEFAULT_FLAGS ] in
  ignore (app#on_activate ~callback:(fun () -> activate app) ());
  exit (app#run 0 None)
]}

{1 Build and run}

{@sh[
dune build
dune exec ./counter.exe
]}

A window opens with a label and a button. Each click updates the label.
Closing the window ends the program.

{1 What the program does}

- {b The application.} {!Ocgtk.Application} starts GTK, runs the main loop,
  and quits when its last window closes. GTK emits its [activate] signal
  when the application starts, and that is where the window is built. The
  ID [org.example.Counter] names the application to the desktop. See
  {{!page-conventions.section-mainloop}The main loop and threads}.
- {b Creating objects.} Each GTK class has a module ({!Ocgtk.Application_window},
  {!Ocgtk.Button}, ...) with its constructors: [Button.new_with_label]
  wraps [gtk_button_new_with_label]. A constructor returns an OCaml object,
  and you call its methods with [#]: [window#set_title].
- {b Signals.} [button#on_clicked ~callback ()] connects a handler to the
  button's [clicked] signal. Don't leave out the final [()]: without it the
  handler is never connected. See {{!page-conventions.section-signals}Signals}.

{1 Next steps}

- {{!page-conventions}The conventions page} explains how the bindings are
  organised and the conventions used for translating them from C to OCaml.
  This includes the class and functional (raw) layers, how classes are
  structured, inheritance and casting, properties, signals, internal memory
  management and how C types map onto OCaml.
- {{!page-modules}The module index} lists every GTK class, interface and
  record. This is the reference documentation generated from GTK's own
  documentation, with cross-referencing links and all the OCaml types
  (note that some aspects still appear in C, such as code samples).
- The {{:https://github.com/chris-armstrong/ocgtk/tree/main/ocgtk/examples}examples}
  include a text editor, a settings panel, a login form and a calculator
  that are worth a quick look to see how to use the libraries.
